1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,440
Last time, the caterpillar turned out to be a larva.

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:06,320
It became a pupa, then a butterfly.

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:09,670
Then Basil found something wriggling in an old pail!

4
00:00:12,483 --> 00:00:15,333
A little dragonfly is hiding in this video.

5
00:00:15,583 --> 00:00:16,823
Can you spot it?

6
00:00:16,944 --> 00:00:19,674
Dragonflies love to perch on the tips of sticks.

7
00:00:19,794 --> 00:00:21,154
Keep your eyes open!

8
00:00:21,284 --> 00:00:23,124
Lock in first with your fingers.

9
00:00:23,394 --> 00:00:25,224
One finger for every point.

10
00:00:25,811 --> 00:00:26,981
Today you'll learn:

11
00:00:27,178 --> 00:00:28,858
The mosquito's life cycle.

12
00:00:29,058 --> 00:00:30,498
Mosquitoes and dengue.

13
00:00:30,699 --> 00:00:32,299
Why we tip out still water.

14
00:00:34,047 --> 00:00:34,927
Round one.

15
00:00:35,227 --> 00:00:37,657
These wriggly things are young mosquitoes.

16
00:00:37,917 --> 00:00:39,187
What comes next?

17
00:00:39,457 --> 00:00:40,637
One, an egg.

18
00:00:40,937 --> 00:00:42,157
Two, a pupa.

19
00:00:42,487 --> 00:00:43,747
Three, an adult.

20
00:00:44,027 --> 00:00:45,507
Show me with your fingers.

21
00:00:50,974 --> 00:00:51,954
Locked in!

22
00:00:52,082 --> 00:00:52,662
Three!

23
00:00:53,032 --> 00:00:57,072
They are baby mosquitoes, so next they fly away as mosquitoes!

24
00:00:57,121 --> 00:00:57,761
Two!

25
00:00:57,891 --> 00:00:59,971
The caterpillar became a pupa first.

26
00:01:00,051 --> 00:01:01,911
I bet the mosquito does that too!

27
00:01:01,959 --> 00:01:02,849
Two.

28
00:01:03,019 --> 00:01:06,419
They look nothing like a mosquito, so I think they change inside

29
00:01:06,439 --> 00:01:07,669
a pupa first.

30
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:09,750
Look closer.

31
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:14,236
Each one hangs upside down, just under the water's surface.

32
00:01:14,256 --> 00:01:16,188
There is a little tube at its tail.

33
00:01:16,208 --> 00:01:19,657
It pokes up through the surface, like a snorkel, to get air!

34
00:01:20,922 --> 00:01:24,392
The mosquito larva is also called a wriggler.

35
00:01:25,662 --> 00:01:28,362
It is called a wriggler because it wriggles, like this!

36
00:01:28,432 --> 00:01:30,622
It is the mosquito's larva, its young.

37
00:01:33,251 --> 00:01:34,551
Be a mosquito egg!

38
00:01:34,881 --> 00:01:35,861
Shrink down.

39
00:01:36,141 --> 00:01:37,081
Quietly.

40
00:01:39,362 --> 00:01:42,062
You are a tiny egg, laid on still water.

41
00:01:42,862 --> 00:01:44,082
Days go by.

42
00:01:44,862 --> 00:01:46,772
You hatch, and you are a wriggler!

43
00:01:47,285 --> 00:01:48,885
You hang from the surface.

44
00:01:49,655 --> 00:01:52,635
You eat tiny bits in the water, you moult, and you grow

45
00:01:52,655 --> 00:01:53,345
bigger.

46
00:01:53,854 --> 00:01:55,684
Then you curl up into a pupa.

47
00:01:56,484 --> 00:01:59,374
You do not eat, but you keep changing inside.

48
00:01:59,881 --> 00:02:01,821
Your case splits at the surface.

49
00:02:02,611 --> 00:02:05,101
You climb out, and you dry your wings.

50
00:02:05,871 --> 00:02:07,631
You are an adult mosquito!

51
00:02:09,358 --> 00:02:11,318
The answer is two: the pupa!

52
00:02:11,628 --> 00:02:12,668
Two fingers?

53
00:02:12,938 --> 00:02:14,148
One finger up!

54
00:02:14,351 --> 00:02:15,601
Say it with me!

55
00:02:16,313 --> 00:02:20,173
A life cycle with four stages includes the egg, the larva, the

56
00:02:20,193 --> 00:02:21,733
pupa and the adult.

57
00:02:25,363 --> 00:02:26,263
Showdown!

58
00:02:26,573 --> 00:02:27,703
Double points.

59
00:02:27,973 --> 00:02:30,163
Basil's homework has one mistake.

60
00:02:30,443 --> 00:02:32,203
Line one, two, or three?

61
00:02:32,503 --> 00:02:33,983
Show me with your fingers.

62
00:02:34,041 --> 00:02:35,801
My homework is on the mosquito!

63
00:02:35,981 --> 00:02:37,571
One: it has four stages.

64
00:02:37,611 --> 00:02:39,221
Two: its larva is a wriggler.

65
00:02:39,351 --> 00:02:41,311
Three: all mosquitoes spread dengue.

66
00:02:46,925 --> 00:02:47,905
Locked in!

67
00:02:48,033 --> 00:02:48,903
Three.

68
00:02:49,083 --> 00:02:51,693
Most mosquitoes do not spread any disease.

69
00:02:51,913 --> 00:02:53,583
Only some kinds do.

70
00:02:55,048 --> 00:02:58,838
Most of the mosquitoes do not spread diseases, but some do.

71
00:02:59,128 --> 00:03:04,428
For example, Aedes albopictus and Aedes aegypti spread dengue.

72
00:03:05,888 --> 00:03:08,998
Dengue is a sickness that gives people a high fever.

73
00:03:09,048 --> 00:03:11,908
Only some mosquitoes carry it, and they pass it on when they

74
00:03:11,928 --> 00:03:12,676
bite.

75
00:03:12,696 --> 00:03:13,786
Three fingers?

76
00:03:14,026 --> 00:03:15,956
Put up two more on your score hand!

77
00:03:16,306 --> 00:03:19,046
But how does the life cycle help to keep us safe?

78
00:03:19,066 --> 00:03:21,856
This flower-pot plate has only a little water.

79
00:03:22,076 --> 00:03:24,596
Surely that is too small for mosquitoes.

80
00:03:25,250 --> 00:03:26,400
Look closer.

81
00:03:26,990 --> 00:03:29,784
This little bit of water has sat still for days.

82
00:03:29,804 --> 00:03:33,054
Mosquito eggs are laid in stagnant water.

83
00:03:33,784 --> 00:03:37,004
Stagnant water is still water that nobody changes.

84
00:03:37,214 --> 00:03:40,098
Even a tiny plate of it is enough for mosquitoes!

85
00:03:40,118 --> 00:03:41,828
So, tip the water out.

86
00:03:42,398 --> 00:03:46,237
No still water means no eggs, no wrigglers and no pupae.

87
00:03:46,257 --> 00:03:47,827
And no new mosquitoes!

88
00:03:47,877 --> 00:03:50,947
At home, we tip out the plates, turn the pails over, and

89
00:03:50,967 --> 00:03:52,967
change the water in the vases.

90
00:03:55,668 --> 00:03:56,828
Say it with me!

91
00:03:57,536 --> 00:04:01,336
We can reduce the number of mosquitoes breeding by removing their breeding

92
00:04:01,356 --> 00:04:03,646
spots in our schools and homes.

93
00:04:06,404 --> 00:04:07,509
Say it with us!

94
00:04:08,169 --> 00:04:09,669
Egg, larva, pupa, adult!

95
00:04:09,749 --> 00:04:10,959
Four stages!

96
00:04:14,569 --> 00:04:16,019
The pupa does not eat!

97
00:04:16,149 --> 00:04:17,549
It changes inside!

98
00:04:20,969 --> 00:04:22,059
Still water?

99
00:04:22,549 --> 00:04:23,591
Tip it out!

100
00:04:28,124 --> 00:04:29,594
Show me your score hand!

101
00:04:29,874 --> 00:04:30,924
More than one?

102
00:04:31,224 --> 00:04:32,584
You beat the Buddies.

103
00:04:32,884 --> 00:04:33,944
Three fingers?

104
00:04:34,214 --> 00:04:35,224
High five!

105
00:04:35,729 --> 00:04:37,429
Did you spot the dragonfly?

106
00:04:37,709 --> 00:04:40,149
It was on the handle of the watering can!

107
00:04:40,286 --> 00:04:41,766
Here is a fun fact!

108
00:04:41,846 --> 00:04:45,816
A baby dragonfly is a nymph that lives underwater, and it gobbles

109
00:04:45,836 --> 00:04:47,546
up mosquito wrigglers!

110
00:04:47,673 --> 00:04:48,953
Tell someone at home!

111
00:04:49,742 --> 00:04:52,272
Next time: a frog and a mosquito!

112
00:04:52,522 --> 00:04:55,942
Both lay their eggs in water, and both have young that look

113
00:04:55,962 --> 00:04:57,402
nothing like the adult.

114
00:04:57,672 --> 00:04:59,502
Are their life cycles the same?

115
00:04:59,792 --> 00:05:00,882
One, yes.

116
00:05:01,142 --> 00:05:02,002
Two, no.

117
00:05:02,292 --> 00:05:03,602
Lock in your answer!

118
00:05:03,876 --> 00:05:06,076
Let's read today's key words together!

119
00:05:06,366 --> 00:05:07,126
Ready?

120
00:05:07,413 --> 00:05:08,173
Wriggler.

121
00:05:09,484 --> 00:05:10,264
Dengue.

122
00:05:11,618 --> 00:05:12,878
Stagnant water.

123
00:05:14,514 --> 00:05:15,454
Well done!

124
00:05:16,436 --> 00:05:17,537
Say it with us!

125
00:05:18,337 --> 00:05:19,087
Ready?

126
00:05:19,211 --> 00:05:19,871
One!

127
00:05:20,211 --> 00:05:20,911
two!

128
00:05:21,216 --> 00:05:22,391
Now you know!
